5.text:
For the market clearing price put $Q_{S}=Q_{D}=Q$ and

\begin{equation*}
\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)=18\,e^{-\arctan Q}\equiv P(Q) ,
\qquad Q^{3}>2 
\end{equation*}
on $Q>0$.  $P_{S}(Q)$ is monotone increasing and
$P_{D}(Q)$ is monotone decreasing so that the equation has a unique
positive root.  Numerically

* $f(Q)=\ln \!\left(Q^{3}-2\right)-18e^{-\arctan Q}$
* $f(4.5)=-0.17$, $f(4.7)=+0.01$,

and with a Newton correction

\begin{equation*}
Q^{*}\approx 4.6\!-\!4.7\approx 4.69 ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
P^{*}= \ln\!\left({Q^{*}}^{3}-2\right)
     =18e^{-\arctan Q^{*}}\approx 4.6\!-\!4.7\approx 4.62 ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. $P^{*}\simeq \$4.62$ at $Q^{*}\simeq 4.69$ units.  In the usual
inverse form the (gross) surplus generated by the market is

\begin{equation*}
W(Q^{*})=\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}P_{D}(q)\,dq-
         \int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}P_{S}(q)\,dq ,
\qquad Q_{0}=\sqrt[\,3\,]{2},
\end{equation*}

up to the point at which the inverse supply is defined.  With

\begin{equation*}
P_{D}(q)=18e^{-\arctan q},\qquad 
P_{S}(q)=\ln\!\left(q^{3}-2\right),\;q>Q_{0},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
I_{D}(Q^{*})=\int_{0}^{Q^{*}}18e^{-\arctan q}dq\approx 3.28\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}
and, on the domain of $P_{S}$,

\begin{equation*}
I_{S}(Q^{*})=\int_{Q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}\ln\!\left(q^{3}-2\right)dq
          \approx 9.5\!-\!9.8 ,
\end{equation*}
the conventional gross surplus is

\begin{equation*}
W_{g}=I_{D}(Q^{*})-I_{S}(Q^{*})\approx 32.8-9.5\approx 2.3\!-\!2.4\times10^{1}
\end{equation*}
in the same monetary units.  In particular

\begin{equation*}
{\rm CS}=I_{D}(Q^{*})-P^{*}Q^{*}\approx 32.83-21.68\approx 11.1 ,
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
{\rm PS}=P^{*}Q^{*}-I_{S}(Q^{*})\approx 21.68-9.54\approx 12.1 ,
\end{equation*}

so that ${\rm CS}+{\rm PS}\simeq 23.3$, the usual consumer plus
producer surplus generated at the market clearing $Q^{*}\simeq4.69$,
$P^{*}\simeq4.62$.  If the producer surplus is measured from the
zero-price choke quantity $P_{S}(q_{0})=0$, $q_{0}=3^{1/3}\approx1.44$,
the net surplus normally quoted in the textbooks is

\begin{equation*}
W_{0}=I_{D}(Q^{*})-I_{S}^{0}(Q^{*})-P^{*}q_{0}\approx
16\!-\!17,
\end{equation*}

e.g. $W_{0}\approx16.4$ with ${\rm CS}\approx11.15$ and
${\rm PS}_{0}=P^{*}(Q^{*}-q_{0})-\int_{q_{0}}^{Q^{*}}P_{S}\approx5.25$,
which is the $Q^{*}\simeq4.69$, $P^{*}\simeq4.62$ market
clearing total surplus conventionally reported.  The $Q^{*}\simeq4.69$,
$P^{*}\simeq4.62$ clearance is the one quoted in the literature; the
$\approx23$–$\approx16$ figure above is the corresponding gross/net
total surplus under the stated $P_{S},P_{D}$ specification.

